  planner_response {
    response: "### \360\237\233\241\357\270\217 Remediation Plan for Accidentally Pushed Secrets\n\nBecause Git preserves full commit history, any API key pushed to a public or shared GitHub repository is considered compromised and should be treated as exposed. Follow this step-by-step remediation guide:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Revoke & Rotate the Exposed Keys Immediately\n\n1. **Gemini API Key:**\n   - Go to [Google AI Studio \342\200\223 Get API Key](https://aistudio.google.com/app/apikey) or the [Google Cloud Credentials Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials).\n   - Locate the compromised key, **Delete / Revoke** it, and create a fresh API key.\n   - In your deployment or local environment, inject the new key via the `GEMINI_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n2. **Firebase API Key:**\n   - In the [Google Cloud Console Credentials](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials), locate your Firebase browser key.\n   - Apply **API Restrictions** (limit it strictly to Firebase Authentication, Cloud Firestore, etc.) and **Application Restrictions** (HTTP Referrer restrictions to your production and localhost domains).\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Remove the Secrets from Your Git History\n\nTo ensure the secret is purged from all previous commits and blobs in your repository, run the following steps on your local machine:\n\n#### Option A: Using `git filter-repo` (Recommended)\n```bash\n# 1. Install git-filter-repo (if not already installed)\npip install git-filter-repo\n\n# 2. Remove the sensitive file from all commits across all branches\ngit filter-repo --path .env --invert-paths\n\n# 3. Force push the cleaned history to GitHub\ngit push origin --force --all\ngit push origin --force --tags\n```\n\n#### Option B: Untrack Current File & Commit\nIf you just committed the file in the latest unpushed/recent state and want to stop tracking it:\n```bash\n# Untrack the file from git index without deleting your local copy\ngit rm --cached .env\n\n# Commit the removal\ngit commit -m \"security: untrack sensitive environment configuration\"\ngit push origin main\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Workspace Protection Confirmed\n\n- **`.gitignore` Verified**: Your repository\'s `.gitignore` is configured to ignore all `.env*` files (except `.env.example`).\n- **Zero-Exposure Architecture**: All Gemini API interactions run through the secure server-side Express proxy (`/api/gemini/*`), ensuring `GEMINI_API_KEY` is never exposed in client browser code or network inspection tabs."
    thinking: "**Formulating Immediate Response**\n\nI\'ve got it! I\'m formulating a clear and actionable response, focusing on the crucial immediate steps. The plan centers on rapid secret revocation and key rotation, as this is the most important part of the response. I\'m building from there.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "### \360\237\233\241\357\270\217 Remediation Plan for Accidentally Pushed Secrets\n\nBecause Git preserves full commit history, any API key pushed to a public or shared GitHub repository is considered compromised and should be treated as exposed. Follow this step-by-step remediation guide:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Revoke & Rotate the Exposed Keys Immediately\n\n1. **Gemini API Key:**\n   - Go to [Google AI Studio \342\200\223 Get API Key](https://aistudio.google.com/app/apikey) or the [Google Cloud Credentials Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials).\n   - Locate the compromised key, **Delete / Revoke** it, and create a fresh API key.\n   - In your deployment or local environment, inject the new key via the `GEMINI_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n2. **Firebase API Key:**\n   - In the [Google Cloud Console Credentials](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials), locate your Firebase browser key.\n   - Apply **API Restrictions** (limit it strictly to Firebase Authentication, Cloud Firestore, etc.) and **Application Restrictions** (HTTP Referrer restrictions to your production and localhost domains).\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Remove the Secrets from Your Git History\n\nTo ensure the secret is purged from all previous commits and blobs in your repository, run the following steps on your local machine:\n\n#### Option A: Using `git filter-repo` (Recommended)\n```bash\n# 1. Install git-filter-repo (if not already installed)\npip install git-filter-repo\n\n# 2. Remove the sensitive file from all commits across all branches\ngit filter-repo --path .env --invert-paths\n\n# 3. Force push the cleaned history to GitHub\ngit push origin --force --all\ngit push origin --force --tags\n```\n\n#### Option B: Untrack Current File & Commit\nIf you just committed the file in the latest unpushed/recent state and want to stop tracking it:\n```bash\n# Untrack the file from git index without deleting your local copy\ngit rm --cached .env\n\n# Commit the removal\ngit commit -m \"security: untrack sensitive environment configuration\"\ngit push origin main\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Workspace Protection Confirmed\n\n- **`.gitignore` Verified**: Your repository\'s `.gitignore` is configured to ignore all `.env*` files (except `.env.example`).\n- **Zero-Exposure Architecture**: All Gemini API interactions run through the secure server-side Express proxy (`/api/gemini/*`), ensuring `GEMINI_API_KEY` is never exposed in client browser code or network inspection tabs."
    thinking_duration {
      nanos: 725354968
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
